  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "c.setup",
      "kind": "framing",
      "text": "The program sets n to 2 and switches on n. Case 2 has no break.",
      "justification": "Read directly from programs/fallthrough.c: line 4 is int n = 2; line 5 is switch (n); lines 8-9 hold case 2 and puts(\"two\") with no break before case 3 on line 10.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "programs/fallthrough.c",
          "url": "programs/fallthrough.c",
          "clause": "lines 4-12"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.match",
      "kind": "rule",
      "text": "A switch jumps to the case label whose value matches the controlling expression.",
      "justification": "C17 6.8.4.2p5: if a converted case value matches the promoted controlling expression, control jumps to the statement following the matched case label. Here n is 2, so control jumps to case 2.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§6.8.4.2 ¶5",
          "quote": "If a converted value matches that of the promoted controlling expression, control jumps to the statement following the matched case label."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.labels",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "A case label is only a label. It does not stop execution.",
      "justification": "C17 6.8.1p4: labels in themselves do not alter the flow of control, which continues unimpeded across them. case 3: is a label, so control flows past it into puts(\"three\").",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§6.8.1 ¶4",
          "quote": "Labels in themselves do not alter the flow of control, which continues unimpeded across them."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.fall",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "With n = 2, the switch runs case 2 and then falls through into case 3, so it prints two, then three.",
      "justification": "Combines c.match (enter at case 2) and c.labels (case 3: does not stop control). The captured run of programs/fallthrough.c prints two on line 1 and three on line 2.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§6.8.4.2 ¶5, §6.8.1 ¶4"
        },
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/fallthrough.c",
          "clause": "stdout lines 1-2"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.p1.l1",
        "f.p1.l2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.break",
      "kind": "rule",
      "text": "A break statement ends the smallest enclosing switch or loop. Here that is the switch, so execution continues after it.",
      "justification": "C17 6.8.6.3p2: a break statement terminates execution of the smallest enclosing switch or iteration statement. The break on line 12 is not inside any loop, so it ends the switch and control continues at line 16; case 4 never runs.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§6.8.6.3 ¶2",
          "quote": "A break statement terminates execution of the smallest enclosing switch or iteration statement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.done",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "After the switch ends, the program prints done.",
      "justification": "puts(\"done\") on line 16 follows the switch; the captured run prints done on line 3.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/fallthrough.c",
          "clause": "stdout line 3"
        }
      ],
      "facts": [
        "f.p1.l3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c.withbreak",
      "kind": "mechanism",
      "text": "With a break after case 2, the switch prints only two, and then the program prints done.",
      "justification": "programs/with_break.c adds break; after puts(\"two\") (line 10). By c.break the switch ends there, so only two comes from the switch; done comes from puts(\"done\") after the switch (line 17). The captured run prints two then done.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "ISO/IEC 9899:2018 (C17), working draft N2176",
          "url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2176.pdf",
          "clause": "§6.8.6.3 ¶2"
        },
        {
          "title": "captured run of programs/with_break.c",
          "clause": "stdout lines 1-2"
        }
      ],
